      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Sixty patients scheduled for septal surgery were assessed preoperatively with regard to their symptoms of nasal obstruction, catarrh and facial pain. They were scored on a linear analogue scale and then re-assessed two months postoperatively. There was a highly significant improvement in scores for all three symptoms (p less than 0.005)."},
